//! Golden-string tests for the Graphviz DOT dump (`src/dump.rs`).
//!
//! # How these goldens were obtained
//!
//! Issue #11 asks for them to be generated by running upstream's `dump` on the
//! same graph in C++ once and pasting the result. **That was not done, and
//! nothing here is evidence of a C++ run:** the development container has no
//! C++ compiler (no `g++`), so the goldens below were *derived by reading*
//! upstream's four `dump`/`_dump` functions -- `taskflow/core/taskflow.hpp`
//! lines 426-580 of Taskflow v4.1.0, commit `45366fe5` -- and applying the
//! identifier substitution documented in `src/dump.rs`: a node prints as
//! `p<arena index>` where upstream prints `p<node address>`, and the top-level
//! graph as `p0`/`cluster_p0` where upstream prints its address.
//!
//! The equivalent C++ is kept with each case so the goldens can be regenerated
//! anywhere a compiler exists. The *only* difference to expect from such a run
//! is in those identifiers; every other byte -- the `compound=true;`, the two
//! spaces per nesting level, the space before `]` in a node line, the trailing
//! newline -- is upstream's own and is asserted here exactly.
//!
//! For the same reason, the issue's "output parses with `dot`" check is **not
//! done**: there is no `graphviz` in the container either. It stays an open
//! item, and it was never part of the gate.
//!
//! The nested-graph goldens added for issue #46 come from the same reading of
//! the same four functions, and carry the same caveat: the module-stack loop
//! of the top-level `_dump` (lines 441-477), the `MODULE` branch of the
//! per-graph one (lines 569-588) and the `SUBFLOW`/`ADOPTED_MODULE` branch of
//! the per-node one (lines 518-551). **No `dot` parse and no C++ run back
//! them either.**
//!
//! Those three branches are also where the identifier substitution has to go
//! further than #11's, because every graph numbers its own nodes from zero
//! while upstream's addresses were unique across all of them. The scheme
//! `src/dump.rs` documents and these goldens pin: the top-level graph keeps
//! the bare `p<index>`, a module graph with id 1 is `cluster_p1` and holds
//! `p1_0`, `p1_1`, ..., and the subgraph of a node `p0` is `cluster_p0_sub`
//! and holds `p0_sub_0`, `p0_sub_1`, ... Upstream's cluster names are
//! addresses too -- a graph's for a module, the owning *node's* for a subflow
//! -- so the `_sub` component is this port's, and it is what keeps the two
//! families from colliding: without it a subflow hanging off the top graph's
//! `p1` and module graph 1 would both claim `cluster_p1` and `p1_0`. Every
//! case below runs its document through `assert_unique_node_ids`, since a
//! collision is exactly the kind of thing a golden read by eye does not catch.
//!
//! The conditioner goldens added for issue #30 come from the same reading and
//! carry the same caveat: the `CONDITION`/`MULTI_CONDITION` case of the shape
//! `switch` (line 497) and the `_is_conditioner()` branch of the successor
//! loop (lines 507-509) of that same `_dump`, with `_is_conditioner()` itself
//! at `taskflow/core/graph.hpp` lines 792-796. **No `dot` parse and no C++ run
//! back them either.**
//!
//! One deliberate difference in how the graphs are *built*: upstream's
//! snippets use `emplace`/`precede`, which is another issue's API, so these
//! tests use the arena primitives, and placeholder handles wherever the handle
//! is not what is under test. That cannot change the output -- a placeholder
//! and a static task both fall through upstream's shape `switch` to `default`
//! and so print the same line -- and `src/dump.rs`'s
//! `a_static_handle_dumps_like_a_placeholder` asserts it. A conditioner is the
//! exception: its handle is exactly what the shape and the edge style key off,
//! so those cases build a real `Handle::Condition`. So are the four handles of
//! #45, for the same reason, and a subflow's subgraph is built by hand on top
//! of that: a body builds one while it runs, the executor does not run a
//! subflow task until #50, and a dump never looks at the body anyway.

use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::fmt;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

use rustflow::{Graph, Handle, SubflowWork, Task, Taskflow, Work};

/// Asserts that no identifier is declared twice in `dot`.
///
/// The identifiers are the one part of the output this port invents rather
/// than copies, and nesting is where an invented scheme can collide -- see the
/// module docs above for the scheme and for the collision it is shaped to
/// avoid. A node declaration is the only line that opens a `[` without being
/// an edge, which is what this reads.
///
/// The empty-taskflow case is the one golden that cannot be checked this way,
/// having no declarations at all, and the trailing assertion is what keeps a
/// case from passing because the parsing above found nothing.
fn assert_unique_node_ids(dot: &str) {
    let mut seen = HashSet::new();
    for line in dot.lines() {
        let line = line.trim();
        if line.contains("->") || !line.ends_with("];") {
            continue;
        }
        let Some((id, _)) = line.split_once('[') else {
            continue;
        };
        assert!(seen.insert(id), "the id {id} is declared twice in\n{dot}");
    }
    assert!(!seen.is_empty(), "no node declaration found in\n{dot}");
}

/// A graph of `names.len()` placeholders, which is what a subflow body would
/// have built had the executor run one yet.
fn child_graph(names: &[&str]) -> Graph {
    let mut graph = Graph::new();
    for name in names {
        graph.add_named_node(*name, Handle::Placeholder);
    }
    graph
}

/// A subflow handle over a subgraph built by hand. The body is the one part of
/// a subflow task a dump never reads.
fn subflow_handle(subgraph: Graph) -> Handle {
    Handle::Subflow {
        work: SubflowWork::new(|_| {}),
        subgraph: Mutex::new(subgraph),
    }
}

/// A taskflow named `name` holding one placeholder named `task`, as the target
/// of a module task.
fn target(name: &str, task: &str) -> Arc<Taskflow> {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name(name);
    taskflow
        .graph_mut()
        .add_named_node(task, Handle::Placeholder);
    Arc::new(taskflow)
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow;
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// The cluster is emitted even with nothing in it: upstream's module-stack
/// `_dump` pushes the top-level graph unconditionally and only the node loop
/// inside it is empty.
#[test]
fn an_empty_taskflow_dumps_an_empty_cluster() {
    let taskflow = Taskflow::new();
    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: p0\";\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow;
/// taskflow.placeholder();
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// Both fallbacks for an absent name show up here: the taskflow's own label
/// and the node's label are each the id that upstream would print as an
/// address.
#[test]
fn one_unnamed_node_falls_back_to_its_id() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::new();
    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.graph_mut().add_node(Handle::Placeholder),
        Task::new(0)
    );
    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: p0\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"p0\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("diamond");
/// auto [a, b, c, d] = taskflow.emplace(
///   [](){}, [](){}, [](){}, [](){}
/// );
/// a.name("a");
/// b.name("b");
/// c.name("c");
/// d.name("d");
/// a.precede(b, c);
/// b.precede(d);
/// c.precede(d);
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// This is the case that pins the interleaving: upstream dumps a node and then
/// that node's own edges before moving to the next node, rather than all nodes
/// and then all edges.
#[test]
fn a_named_diamond_dumps_each_node_with_its_own_edges() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("diamond");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let a = graph.add_named_node("a", Handle::Placeholder);
    let b = graph.add_named_node("b", Handle::Placeholder);
    let c = graph.add_named_node("c", Handle::Placeholder);
    let d = graph.add_named_node("d", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(a, b);
    graph.add_edge(a, c);
    graph.add_edge(b, d);
    graph.add_edge(c, d);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: diamond\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"a\" ];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p0 -> p2;\n",
            "    p1[label=\"b\" ];\n",
            "    p1 -> p3;\n",
            "    p2[label=\"c\" ];\n",
            "    p2 -> p3;\n",
            "    p3[label=\"d\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// `Display` is the same document, so `{taskflow}` and `println!` need no
/// `dump()` call. Asserted against the diamond golden's own body so the two
/// paths cannot drift apart.
#[test]
fn display_writes_the_same_document() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("displayed");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let a = graph.add_named_node("a", Handle::Placeholder);
    let b = graph.add_node(Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(a, b);

    assert_eq!(
        format!("{taskflow}"),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: displayed\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"a\" ];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p1[label=\"p1\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_eq!(taskflow.dump(), taskflow.to_string());
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// `Display` over a graph with nested clusters, which is the part of the dump
/// that is generic over the writer and has somewhere to recurse into: a
/// subflow subgraph and a module target both hang off the top-level graph
/// here, so the `{taskflow}` route walks the module stack and the subgraph
/// branch rather than the flat node loop the case above drives.
///
/// The document a writer gets cannot depend on which writer it is, so the
/// assertion is against `dump()`'s own output -- the same pinning
/// [`display_writes_the_same_document`] does, over the shape that has two more
/// code paths to disagree in.
#[test]
fn display_writes_the_same_document_for_a_nested_graph() {
    let target = target("target", "x");

    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("nested");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let spawner = graph.add_named_node("spawner", subflow_handle(child_graph(&["c0"])));
    let module = graph.add_named_node("call-target", Handle::Module(target));
    graph.add_edge(spawner, module);

    let document = taskflow.dump();
    assert_eq!(format!("{taskflow}"), document);
    assert_eq!(taskflow.to_string(), document);

    // Both nested branches really are in what was compared, so this is not
    // two writers agreeing on a flat graph.
    assert!(document.contains("subgraph cluster_p0_sub {\n"));
    assert!(document.contains("subgraph cluster_p1 {\n"));
    assert_unique_node_ids(&document);
}

/// The graph-level entry point emits the cluster body alone -- upstream's
/// per-graph `_dump`, which is a fragment and not a document.
#[test]
fn a_graph_dumps_only_the_cluster_body() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("diamond");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let a = graph.add_named_node("a", Handle::Placeholder);
    let b = graph.add_named_node("b", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(a, b);

    let mut body = String::new();
    taskflow
        .graph()
        .dump_into(&mut body)
        .expect("writing to a String cannot fail");

    assert_eq!(
        body,
        concat!(
            "    p0[label=\"a\" ];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p1[label=\"b\" ];\n",
        )
    );
    // And it is literally the document minus the wrapper.
    assert!(taskflow.dump().contains(&body));
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// A writer that refuses everything, for the error path of a dump.
struct Refusing;

impl fmt::Write for Refusing {
    fn write_str(&mut self, _s: &str) -> fmt::Result {
        Err(fmt::Error)
    }
}

/// `Taskflow::dump_into` is the entry point `dump` and `Display` are both
/// built on: it writes the whole document into a writer the caller already
/// holds, appending to what is there rather than replacing it, and hands
/// back whatever the writer refused.
///
/// Upstream's `Taskflow::dump(std::ostream&)` is the same entry point. The
/// error is this port's own: a `fmt::Write` reports a refused write, where
/// an `ostream` sets a bit upstream never reads.
#[test]
fn dump_into_appends_the_document_and_reports_the_writers_error() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("into");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let a = graph.add_named_node("a", Handle::Placeholder);
    let b = graph.add_named_node("b", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(a, b);

    let document = taskflow.dump();

    let mut out = String::from("before\n");
    taskflow
        .dump_into(&mut out)
        .expect("writing to a String cannot fail");
    assert_eq!(
        out,
        format!("before\n{document}"),
        "the document went after what the writer already held"
    );

    // The same writer again: two documents, one after the other, which is
    // what makes this the form a caller streams several graphs into.
    taskflow
        .dump_into(&mut out)
        .expect("writing to a String cannot fail");
    assert_eq!(out, format!("before\n{document}{document}"));

    assert!(
        taskflow.dump_into(&mut Refusing).is_err(),
        "the writer's own refusal comes back to the caller"
    );
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("branch");
/// auto [cond, yes, no] = taskflow.emplace(
///   [](){ return 0; }, [](){}, [](){}
/// );
/// cond.name("cond");
/// yes.name("yes");
/// no.name("no");
/// cond.precede(yes, no);
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// Both halves of the conditioner format in one document: the diamond on the
/// node line, and one dashed edge per successor labelled with the integer the
/// condition returns to take it -- so the labels are positions in the
/// successor list, `"0"` then `"1"`, and not the successors' own ids.
#[test]
fn a_conditioner_dumps_as_a_diamond_with_dashed_position_labels() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("branch");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let cond = graph.add_named_node("cond", Handle::Condition(Work::new(|| 0)));
    let yes = graph.add_named_node("yes", Handle::Placeholder);
    let no = graph.add_named_node("no", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(cond, yes);
    graph.add_edge(cond, no);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: branch\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"cond\" shape=diamond color=black fillcolor=aquamarine style=filled];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1 [style=dashed label=\"0\"];\n",
            "    p0 -> p2 [style=dashed label=\"1\"];\n",
            "    p1[label=\"yes\" ];\n",
            "    p2[label=\"no\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("mixed");
/// auto [init, cond, body] = taskflow.emplace(
///   [](){}, [](){ return 0; }, [](){}
/// );
/// init.name("init");
/// cond.name("cond");
/// body.name("body");
/// init.precede(cond);
/// cond.precede(body);
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// The dashed style is per *source* node, not per graph: upstream asks
/// `node->_is_conditioner()` inside the successor loop, so a static task in a
/// graph that also holds a conditioner still writes the bare `p0 -> p1;` of
/// the M1 goldens above.
#[test]
fn a_static_task_keeps_its_plain_edges_beside_a_conditioner() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("mixed");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let init = graph.add_named_node("init", Handle::Static(Work::new(|| {})));
    let cond = graph.add_named_node("cond", Handle::Condition(Work::new(|| 0)));
    let body = graph.add_named_node("body", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(init, cond);
    graph.add_edge(cond, body);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: mixed\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"init\" ];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p1[label=\"cond\" shape=diamond color=black fillcolor=aquamarine style=filled];\n",
            "    p1 -> p2 [style=dashed label=\"0\"];\n",
            "    p2[label=\"body\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("do-while");
/// auto [A, B, C] = taskflow.emplace(
///   [](){}, [](){ return 0; }, [](){}
/// );
/// A.name("A");
/// B.name("B");
/// C.name("C");
/// A.precede(B);
/// B.precede(B, C);
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// Upstream's do-while loop: the conditioner's first successor is itself. The
/// dump has nothing to say about that -- it walks the successor list and
/// labels by position either way -- which is the point of asserting it, since
/// a self-loop is the one shape a reader expects the writer to special-case.
#[test]
fn a_conditioner_self_loop_dumps_as_a_dashed_edge_to_itself() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("do-while");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let a = graph.add_named_node("A", Handle::Placeholder);
    let b = graph.add_named_node("B", Handle::Condition(Work::new(|| 0)));
    let c = graph.add_named_node("C", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(a, b);
    graph.add_edge(b, b);
    graph.add_edge(b, c);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: do-while\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"A\" ];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p1[label=\"B\" shape=diamond color=black fillcolor=aquamarine style=filled];\n",
            "    p1 -> p1 [style=dashed label=\"0\"];\n",
            "    p1 -> p2 [style=dashed label=\"1\"];\n",
            "    p2[label=\"C\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow target("target");
/// target.emplace([](){}).name("x");
///
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("composer");
/// tf::Task named = taskflow.composed_of(target);
/// named.name("call-target");
/// tf::Task unnamed = taskflow.composed_of(target);
/// named.precede(unnamed);
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// One target composed twice: `Dumper::visited` is keyed by the graph, so the
/// second module task finds an id instead of handing out a new one, and the
/// document holds two `box3d` nodes over one cluster. The cluster's label
/// comes from whichever task visited first -- the named one here -- and the
/// unnamed task falls back to its own id plus the ` [m1]` that says which
/// cluster it means.
#[test]
fn two_module_tasks_on_one_target_share_one_cluster() {
    let target = target("target", "x");

    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("composer");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let named = graph.add_named_node("call-target", Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&target)));
    let unnamed = graph.add_node(Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&target)));
    graph.add_edge(named, unnamed);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: composer\";\n",
            "    p0[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"call-target\"];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p1[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"p1 [m1]\"];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p1 {\n",
            "      label=\"call-target\";\n",
            "      p1_0[label=\"x\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow f0("f0");
/// f0.emplace([](){}).name("leaf");
///
/// tf::Taskflow f1("f1");
/// f1.composed_of(f0).name("call-f0");
///
/// tf::Taskflow f2("f2");
/// f2.composed_of(f1).name("call-f1");
/// std::cout << f2.dump();
/// ```
///
/// A module graph's own module tasks push in their turn, so the stack carries
/// the composition down as far as it goes: three graphs, three clusters, and
/// ids 0, 1, 2 in the order they were first reached. The deepening indentation
/// of the sibling clusters is upstream's too -- it writes a module cluster at
/// the level of the task that composed it, which makes a sibling *look*
/// nested; the braces are where the nesting really is.
#[test]
fn a_two_level_composition_dumps_a_cluster_per_graph_in_visit_order() {
    let f0 = target("f0", "leaf");

    let mut f1 = Taskflow::with_name("f1");
    f1.graph_mut()
        .add_named_node("call-f0", Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&f0)));
    let f1 = Arc::new(f1);

    let mut f2 = Taskflow::with_name("f2");
    f2.graph_mut()
        .add_named_node("call-f1", Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&f1)));

    assert_eq!(
        f2.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: f2\";\n",
            "    p0[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"call-f1\"];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p1 {\n",
            "      label=\"call-f1\";\n",
            "      p1_0[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"call-f0\"];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "      subgraph cluster_p2 {\n",
            "        label=\"call-f0\";\n",
            "        p2_0[label=\"leaf\" ];\n",
            "      }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&f2.dump());
}

/// A module task built through [`FlowBuilder::composed_of`] rather than by
/// hand, dumping exactly as the raw `Handle::Module` cases above do: this
/// port's own case, for issue #181, since every golden above builds its
/// module node with [`Graph::add_node`] by the file's own convention and none
/// of them exercises the builder a caller actually uses.
///
/// Named `"pipeline"`, standing for the graph a pipeline owns once #182
/// lands -- a `Taskflow` like any other, named by the user or left to fall
/// back to `"pipeline"`, composed into another graph the same way any module
/// target is.
#[test]
fn a_taskflow_composed_through_the_builder_renders_its_cluster() {
    use rustflow::FlowBuilder;

    let mut pipeline = Taskflow::with_name("pipeline");
    pipeline
        .graph_mut()
        .add_named_node("stage", Handle::Placeholder);
    let pipeline = Arc::new(pipeline);

    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("composer");
    let module = taskflow.composed_of(Arc::clone(&pipeline));
    taskflow.task(module).name("call-pipeline");

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: composer\";\n",
            "    p0[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"call-pipeline\"];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p1 {\n",
            "      label=\"call-pipeline\";\n",
            "      p1_0[label=\"stage\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow a("a");
/// a.emplace([](){}).name("ax");
/// tf::Taskflow b("b");
/// b.emplace([](){}).name("bx");
///
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("two");
/// taskflow.composed_of(a).name("call-a");
/// taskflow.composed_of(b).name("call-b");
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// `Dumper::stack` is a stack, and upstream's loop pops it, so two targets
/// composed by one graph write their clusters in the reverse of the order they
/// were pushed: `cluster_p2` before `cluster_p1`. The ids are still visit
/// order -- `a` was reached first and is 1 -- which is the asymmetry worth
/// pinning, since reading either half alone suggests the other.
#[test]
fn two_module_targets_dump_their_clusters_in_reverse_push_order() {
    let a = target("a", "ax");
    let b = target("b", "bx");

    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("two");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    graph.add_named_node("call-a", Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&a)));
    graph.add_named_node("call-b", Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&b)));

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: two\";\n",
            "    p0[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"call-a\"];\n",
            "    p1[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"call-b\"];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p2 {\n",
            "      label=\"call-b\";\n",
            "      p2_0[label=\"bx\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p1 {\n",
            "      label=\"call-a\";\n",
            "      p1_0[label=\"ax\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("dynamic");
/// tf::Task spawner = taskflow.emplace([](tf::Subflow& sf){
///   tf::Task left = sf.emplace([](){});
///   tf::Task right = sf.emplace([](){});
///   left.name("left");
///   right.name("right");
///   left.precede(right);
///   sf.retain(true);                  // or the subgraph is cleared on join
/// });
/// spawner.name("spawner");
/// tf::Task after = taskflow.emplace([](){});
/// after.name("after");
/// spawner.precede(after);
/// tf::Executor().run(taskflow).wait();  // the children exist only after a run
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// The nested half of the format, in one document: the cluster sits *inside*
/// the owning node's cluster, after that node's own edges, and carries
/// `color=blue` and a `Subflow: <name>` label. What follows it is upstream's
/// one cluster-level join edge -- from the subgraph's first node, not from all
/// of them -- dashed, blue, and clipped to the cluster boundary by `ltail`,
/// which is what the document's `compound=true` is there for.
#[test]
fn a_subflow_subgraph_dumps_as_a_nested_cluster_with_a_join_edge() {
    let mut subgraph = child_graph(&["left", "right"]);
    subgraph.add_edge(Task::new(0), Task::new(1));

    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("dynamic");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let spawner = graph.add_named_node("spawner", subflow_handle(subgraph));
    let after = graph.add_named_node("after", Handle::Placeholder);
    graph.add_edge(spawner, after);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: dynamic\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"spawner\" ];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p0_sub {\n",
            "      label=\"Subflow: spawner\";\n",
            "      color=blue;\n",
            "      p0_sub_0[label=\"left\" ];\n",
            "      p0_sub_0 -> p0_sub_1;\n",
            "      p0_sub_1[label=\"right\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "    p0_sub_0 -> p0 [ltail=cluster_p0_sub style=dashed color=blue];\n",
            "    p1[label=\"after\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Graph g;
/// tf::FlowBuilder builder{g};
/// builder.emplace([](){}).name("a");
/// builder.emplace([](){}).name("b");
///
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("adopted");
/// taskflow.adopt(std::move(g));     // left unnamed
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// An adopted module shares the subflow's branch of upstream's per-node
/// `_dump` and differs from it in exactly one word of the label. The node is
/// unnamed here, so both fallbacks inside a nested cluster show up at once:
/// the cluster is labelled with the owning node's id and the children with
/// their own.
#[test]
fn an_adopted_module_subgraph_dumps_as_a_nested_cluster() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("adopted");
    taskflow
        .graph_mut()
        .add_node(Handle::AdoptedModule(child_graph(&["a", "b"])));

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: adopted\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"p0\" ];\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p0_sub {\n",
            "      label=\"AdoptedModule: p0\";\n",
            "      color=blue;\n",
            "      p0_sub_0[label=\"a\" ];\n",
            "      p0_sub_1[label=\"b\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "    p0_sub_0 -> p0 [ltail=cluster_p0_sub style=dashed color=blue];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("dynamic");
/// taskflow.emplace([](tf::Subflow& sf){
///   sf.emplace([](){});             // no retain(true), and no run either
/// }).name("spawner");
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// Upstream guards the whole branch with `if(!g.empty())`, so a subflow task
/// whose subgraph holds nothing dumps as a plain node and nothing else: no
/// cluster, no `color=blue`, no join edge. That is what a subflow looks like
/// before the run that builds it, and after a run that joined it without
/// `retain(true)` -- which is why upstream's `dump` documentation says to do
/// both.
#[test]
fn an_empty_subflow_subgraph_dumps_no_cluster() {
    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("dynamic");
    taskflow
        .graph_mut()
        .add_named_node("spawner", subflow_handle(Graph::new()));

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: dynamic\";\n",
            "    p0[label=\"spawner\" ];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}

/// Upstream:
///
/// ```cpp
/// tf::Taskflow target("target");
/// target.emplace([](){}).name("x");
///
/// tf::Taskflow taskflow("mixed");
/// tf::Task composed = taskflow.composed_of(target);   // left unnamed
/// tf::Task spawner = taskflow.emplace([](tf::Subflow& sf){
///   sf.emplace([](){}).name("left");
///   sf.emplace([](){}).name("right");
///   sf.retain(true);
/// });
/// spawner.name("spawner");
/// composed.precede(spawner);
/// tf::Executor().run(taskflow).wait();
/// std::cout << taskflow.dump();
/// ```
///
/// The case the identifier scheme is shaped around, and the reason the `_sub`
/// component exists at all. Upstream names a module cluster after the graph's
/// address and a subflow cluster after the owning *node's*, which cannot
/// collide; the small ids that stand in for both here can. A module graph that
/// is given id 1 and a subflow hanging off the top graph's `p1` would each
/// claim `cluster_p1` and `p1_0` under the obvious scheme -- so the subflow's
/// are `cluster_p1_sub` and `p1_sub_0` instead, and the two families cannot
/// meet. The unnamed module task also shows the cluster-label fallback the
/// first case did not: with no task name to borrow, the cluster is labelled
/// `m1`.
#[test]
fn a_module_graph_and_a_subflow_keep_their_identifiers_apart() {
    let target = target("target", "x");

    let mut taskflow = Taskflow::with_name("mixed");
    let graph = taskflow.graph_mut();
    let composed = graph.add_node(Handle::Module(Arc::clone(&target)));
    let spawner = graph.add_named_node("spawner", subflow_handle(child_graph(&["left", "right"])));
    graph.add_edge(composed, spawner);

    assert_eq!(
        taskflow.dump(),
        concat!(
            "digraph Taskflow {\n",
            "  compound=true;\n",
            "  subgraph cluster_p0 {\n",
            "    label=\"Taskflow: mixed\";\n",
            "    p0[shape=box3d, color=blue, label=\"p0 [m1]\"];\n",
            "    p0 -> p1;\n",
            "    p1[label=\"spawner\" ];\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p1_sub {\n",
            "      label=\"Subflow: spawner\";\n",
            "      color=blue;\n",
            "      p1_sub_0[label=\"left\" ];\n",
            "      p1_sub_1[label=\"right\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "    p1_sub_0 -> p1 [ltail=cluster_p1_sub style=dashed color=blue];\n",
            "  }\n",
            "    subgraph cluster_p1 {\n",
            "      label=\"m1\";\n",
            "      p1_0[label=\"x\" ];\n",
            "    }\n",
            "}\n",
        )
    );
    assert_unique_node_ids(&taskflow.dump());
}
